# tmux-share.tmpfiles: runtime files for tmpfiles.d(5).
#
# Install as /etc/tmpfiles.d/tmux-share.conf (or /usr/lib/tmpfiles.d/). The
# group tmux-share comes from tmux-share.sysusers and must exist first; with
# systemd, systemd-sysusers runs before systemd-tmpfiles-setup at boot.
#
# Fields: Type Path Mode User Group Age Argument. Every mode below is written
# out in full. A "-" mode would mean "use the default", which for a created
# directory is derived from the creator's umask; a boundary that depends on
# the umask of whoever happened to run tmpfiles is not a boundary. Age is "-"
# throughout so /run cleanup never ages these files away under a live server,
# and the Argument is "-" because the files are created empty. All seven
# fields are always written, so every rule has the same shape.
# Nothing here is world-readable or world-writable: the "other" bits are zero
# on every rule.
#
# THE SOCKET HAS NO RULE HERE, ON PURPOSE. tmux creates its socket itself,
# owner-only (mode 0600, the service account), and connecting to a unix socket needs write
# permission on it, so that is the boundary that keeps the reader group from
# driving the shell even though it can traverse the directory the socket is in.
# A tmpfiles rule that pre-created the socket path would have to pick a mode,
# and any mode wider than tmux's own would undo that boundary before tmux ever
# ran. Leaving the path alone means the only thing that can ever make the
# socket is the process that owns it, with its own restrictive mode. The same reasoning covers the other runtime markers
# (.notready-since, .rebuild-required): the scripts create them, owned by the service account.

# OWNER. "root" in the rules below is the service account, which is root unless
# install.sh --user NAME renders this file with NAME in its place (the lock's
# group becomes NAME's primary group). The NixOS module generates its own rules
# from services.tmux-share.user and is kept in step by tests/parity.
#
# The runtime directory: 0710 root:tmux-share. Group "x" without "r" means a
# member of tmux-share can open a path it already knows inside the directory
# (the transcript) but cannot list the directory, so it cannot enumerate what
# else is there. Other: no access at all.
d /run/tmux-share 0710 root tmux-share - -

# The transcript, the reader's contract: 0640 root:tmux-share. The owner writes it;
# the group may read it and nothing more (no group write, so the reader cannot
# alter or truncate the record).
f /run/tmux-share/transcript.log 0640 root tmux-share - -

# The advisory lock: 0600 root:root (user:user for another account), and deliberately NOT group-readable.
# flock(2) needs only read access to the file, so a lock the reader could open
# is a lock the reader could hold, stalling the taps and the trimmer that wait
# on it. It lives outside the runtime directory, in /run itself, so it is not
# reachable through the directory the reader can traverse.
f /run/tmux-share.lock 0600 root root - -
